From: Bernhard Rosenkränzer <bero@lindev.ch>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 14:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] fix: SIGILL on non-AVX2 CPUs, SIGBUS mmap, rg thread races

1. Illegal Instruction on older hardware:
   perf_memmem always called the target("avx2") path with no runtime
   CPU check.  Use __builtin_cpu_supports("avx2") and fall back to
   glibc memmem.  (rg.cpp already had runtime dispatch; only perf.c
   was wrong.)

2. SIGBUS in perf_try_mmap / search_file_ex:
   Patch 0008 rounded map size up, but entire pages past EOF still
   have no file backing under MAP_PRIVATE — kernel delivers SIGBUS.
   Map an anonymous region then overlay the file with MAP_FIXED so
   the DFA sentinel byte always lands on anonymous zeros.

3. SIGSEGV in should_ignore_path_reg:
   Parallel walkers read g_ignore_reg_by_dir without locking while
   ensure_dir_ignores_registered rehashes under the writer lock.
   Use shared_mutex; readers take shared_lock.

4. SIGSEGV/SIGABRT in dfaexec_main / EGexecute / Pexecute:
   A single global g_pat is shared across parallel search workers.
   GNU DFA mutates state (state_index, trans tables) and PCRE match
   data is not thread-safe.  Serialize those engines via g_exec_call;
   Fexecute/kwset stays lock-free.

5. SIGSEGV on rg -m / --max-count:
   Short option string listed "m" without ":", so getopt never set
   optarg and atoll(optarg) crashed.  Use "m:" and null-check optarg.

Also lock g_git_worktree_cache and snapshot VCS roots into TLS so
pointers are not invalidated by rehash.

